        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-20T10:11:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There is a `/proc` api in the C version of ashmem, is that not being emulated in the rust version as well?\n\nAnd do you all have some sort of document saying what you want to use this new api for?  Having it be present for all userspace builds, and then somehow interacting with it on a running system must imply that there\u0027s a userspace tool here for it, right?  😊\n\nand if you want to use a sysctl, sure, but again, why not just a control function on the misc device itself?  No new rust infrastructure will be needed to be built at the very least, AND you should already have access to the proper device node through the selinux protection rules, so no need to modify/add/remove them as well.\n\nBut again, some sort of documet for how you all think this will be used would be great to have, especially for a new user/kernel api that you are wanting to add to all devices.",
      "parentUuid": "983dfc28_54912949",
      "revId": "a5a0a60517448e3709f33dbf4c3e15a5cf3d246d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "68c56cff_22247dde",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 28
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1934715
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-20T16:58:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Are you referring to this callback [1]? If so, all that does is to serve as a hook to add more information to the output of `/proc/pid/fdinfo/fd` when fd corresponds to an ashmem buffer; it\u0027s not something we use to configure the driver. `/proc/pid/fdinfo/fd` exists for all fds as well, so it\u0027s not a node that ashmem exposes.\n\nThe initial thought was to have these sysfs nodes and leverage the rc scripts that already run very early during init to write to the nodes to configure the ashmem driver to behave differently than the default behavior. This would be less involved than introducing a new userspace process that would have to be launched early during boot to use `ioctl()` to configure the driver.\n\nWe don\u0027t have a document for this, but I\u0027m happy to draw one up and share one. However, I\u0027d just like to emphasize that these sysfs nodes are not meant to be used in production or by any clients. They are solely meant for us to configure the driver\u0027s behavior to slowly approximate memfd\u0027s behavior on dogfood builds so we can safely deprecate these features. Once we\u0027ve determined that these features can be deprecated, we plan on removing these toggles.\n\n[1] https://cs.android.com/android/kernel/superproject/+/common-android-mainline:common/drivers/staging/android/ashmem.c;l\u003d917",
      "parentUuid": "c85f4b72_6d45f448",
      "revId": "a5a0a60517448e3709f33dbf4c3e15a5cf3d246d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c021e90a_4e0793dc",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 28
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-20T17:18:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Normally I\u0027d be ok with adding a sysfs file for this type of thing, but this really is an abuse of sysfs, don\u0027t you agree?  Also, the issue is that now you have to add a bunch of kobject and sysfs \"raw\" bindings that shouldn\u0027t be required just because this is in rust.\n\nYour rc scripts can do the same thing to write to a `/dev/ashmem_debug_control_will_be_removed_soon_ignore_this` device node just as easily as they can to a sysfs file.  And bonus, the rust bindings to do that is already present in the tree so your code will be much smaller overall 😊",
      "parentUuid": "68c56cff_22247dde",
      "revId": "a5a0a60517448e3709f33dbf4c3e15a5cf3d246d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
